.TH "BASEAUDIOCONTEXT.CURRENTTIME" "3JS" "September 1, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
BaseAudioContext.currentTime \- BaseAudioContext: currentTime property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[CR]currentTime\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]BaseAudioContext\f[R] interface returns a double representing the
elapsed time in the context\(cqs audio timeline in seconds that can be
used for scheduling audio playback, visualizing timelines, etc.
It starts at 0.
.PP
While the context is running, this value advances in increments of one
audio rendering block, or \f[I]render quantum\f[R], and represents the
start of the next block to be processed.
For a block of 128 sample frames, this is approximately 2.9 ms at a
\f[CR]sampleRate\f[R] of 44.1 kHz, or 2.7 ms at 48 kHz.
Repeated reads can return the same value until the next block is
processed.
.PP
This audio timeline is separate from the system clock used by
\f[CR]Date.now()\f[R].
It stops advancing while the context is suspended.
For an \f[CR]OfflineAudioContext\f[R], it advances as audio is rendered,
independently of elapsed real time.
.SH VALUE
A floating\-point number representing the current time in seconds.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] audioCtx = \f[B]new\f[R] AudioContext();
\f[I]// Older webkit/blink browsers require a prefix\f[R]

\f[I]// \&...\f[R]

console.log(audioCtx.currentTime);
.EE
.SH REDUCED TIME PRECISION
To offer protection against timing attacks and fingerprinting, the
precision of \f[CR]audioCtx.currentTime\f[R] may be reduced depending on
browser settings.
.PP
The value of \f[CR]audioCtx.currentTime\f[R] is based on the number of
audio sample frames processed.
In Chrome and Safari, the browser does not apply additional timer
rounding to this value.
.PP
In Firefox, the \f[CR]privacy.reduceTimerPrecision\f[R] preference is
enabled by default and uses a rounding interval of 1 ms, or 0.02 ms in
cross\-origin\-isolated contexts.
However, Firefox first compares the duration of a 128\-frame audio block
with the interval configured by
\f[CR]privacy.resistFingerprinting.reduceTimerPrecision.microseconds\f[R]
(1 ms by default), regardless of cross\-origin isolation.
If the block duration is greater, Firefox skips timer rounding.
At common sample rates such as 44.1 kHz and 48 kHz, the default value
therefore follows the audio block timing rather than being rounded.
.PP
If \f[CR]privacy.resistFingerprinting\f[R] is enabled, the rounding
interval is 16.667 ms or the interval configured by
\f[CR]privacy.resistFingerprinting.reduceTimerPrecision.microseconds\f[R],
whichever is larger.
In this case, the audio block duration check also uses this larger
interval.
.PP
For example, these are possible values in Firefox:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Audio block timing at 48 kHz with default settings\f[R]
audioCtx.currentTime;
\f[I]// Might be:\f[R]
\f[I]// 0.0026666666666666666\f[R]
\f[I]// 0.005333333333333333\f[R]
\f[I]// 0.008\f[R]
\f[I]// \&...\f[R]

\f[I]// Reduced time precision with \(gaprivacy.resistFingerprinting\(ga enabled\f[R]
audioCtx.currentTime;
\f[I]// Might be:\f[R]
\f[I]// 0.050001\f[R]
\f[I]// 0.066668\f[R]
\f[I]// 0.083335\f[R]
\f[I]// \&...\f[R]
.EE
